#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Fail when a change removes a lot of code without saying anywhere that it
# meant to.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS
# ---------------
# On 2026-08-10 four deliberate removals of LOCAL subsystems surfaced in one
# day -- client-side computer-use dispatch (`9765692e1`), the InitProject
# wizard (`b0b1faef9`), the whole LSP stack (`efcaa42b8`), and
# PersistedWorkspace + codebase indexing (`d84dd8e4d`). Every one was found by
# an agent doing unrelated work. Every one was recorded in NEITHER DECLINED.md
# NOR TODO.md. Two of them had to be restored; a third is still an open
# maintainer question. See TODO.md's "Four deliberate removals" section.
#
# The parity audit could not have caught them: it keys on pin tests, and a
# removed local subsystem carries few or none. The compiler could not catch
# them either -- a removal that compiles is, to the compiler, correct. Nothing
# in this project forced a removal to be WRITTEN DOWN, so the fork spent months
# re-deriving gaps it had created on purpose and then forgotten.
#
# This is the only guard in script/ that prevents future debt rather than
# measuring past debt. It deliberately catches none of the four above -- they
# predate it. It exists so there is never a fifth.
#
# HOW IT WORKS
# ------------
# Diffs the change under review against its base and asks two questions. Either
# one firing is enough:
#
#   T1  deletion-dominant change: at least LINES_REMOVED_LIMIT lines removed
#       across the guarded paths, AND at least twice as many lines removed as
#       added. Rename detection (-M) is on, so moving a file is not a deletion.
#
#   T2  bulk whole-file removal: at least WHOLE_FILE_LINES_LIMIT lines removed
#       by files deleted OUTRIGHT, regardless of what the change adds
#       elsewhere. T2 exists because T1 alone is defeated by bundling: see the
#       measurement below.
#
# Paths matched by script/large_deletion_allowlist.txt are excluded BEFORE
# either question is asked -- lockfiles, prose, agent scratch, build output --
# so they can never be part of why a trigger fired.
#
# If a trigger fires the change must EXPLAIN itself. Either of these clears it:
#
#   * the change description (commit messages in BASE..HEAD, plus $PR_TITLE and
#     $PR_BODY when CI supplies them) mentions `DECLINED.md`; or
#   * that description carries a `Large-deletion:` trailer line with a
#     non-empty reason -- an issue number, a DECLINED.md row, a sentence.
#
# The bar is ONE LINE, on purpose. This guard does not adjudicate whether a
# removal is right -- it cannot, and pretending to would make it an obstacle
# people route around. It only forces the removal to become a visible,
# reviewable claim instead of a silent diff nobody read to the bottom of.
#
# THRESHOLDS, AND THE MEASUREMENT THAT SET THEM (2026-08-17)
# ----------------------------------------------------------
# Measured over all 2,295 non-merge commits in this repository's history
# (2026-04-28 .. 2026-08-17), counting only the guarded paths.
#
# Removed lines per commit are extremely skewed: p50=5, p75=32, p90=153,
# p95=420, p98=1797, max=27,380. So any round number under ~500 sits inside
# ordinary churn.
#
#   LINES_REMOVED_LIMIT=750 is the largest round value that still catches the
#   smallest of the four removals this guard exists for: `9765692e1` is
#   +25/-936. 750 leaves ~20% headroom under it, and only 75 of the 2,295
#   commits (3.3%) remove that much at all -- i.e. it sits at about p96.7.
#
#   WHOLE_FILE_LINES_LIMIT=1500 is set against `44f71a6cc` ("update website"),
#   the one commit in this history that removes 27 files / 2,988 lines outright
#   while adding 12,776 elsewhere -- the bundling shape T1 structurally cannot
#   see. One observed instance is a sample of one, so pinning the limit just
#   under 2,988 would be overfitting; 1,500 is roughly half, and costs exactly
#   TWO extra firings across the whole history versus 2,000 (both of them
#   genuine cloud-sweep removals). Whole-file deletion is rare enough to carry
#   a low limit: only 113 of the 2,295 commits (4.9%) delete any guarded file
#   at all, and 33 exceed 1,500 such lines.
#
# Combined firing rate on real history:
#
#   * all 2,295 commits ................ 57 fire (2.5%)
#   * 2026-06 onward (1,600 commits) ...  7 fire (0.44%)
#   * 2026-08 alone (1,256 commits) ....  3 fire (0.24%)
#
# Reading the 57: the overwhelming majority are literally titled as removals
# ("Remove codebase indexing runtime", "Retire shared session cloud protocol",
# "remove the fork-original SSH Manager feature"). They are the de-clouding
# sweep -- exactly the class this guard wants stated out loud. In the whole
# post-sweep era (2026-06 onward) SEVEN commits fire and every one of the seven
# is a deliberate subsystem removal by its own subject line. That is a 0.44%
# firing rate with, as far as this measurement can tell, no false positives.
#
# All four of the 2026-08-10 removals fire. Three fire on both triggers;
# `9765692e1` (+25/-936, only 208 lines in outright-deleted files) fires on T1
# alone, which is what fixes LINES_REMOVED_LIMIT at 750 rather than higher.
#
# WHAT WAS TRIED AND REJECTED WHILE BUILDING THIS
# -----------------------------------------------
# A guard that cries wolf on legitimate refactors is disabled within a week, so
# three simpler designs were measured first and all three were thrown out:
#
#   1. Raw removed-lines alone, no add/remove ratio. Fires on every large
#      rewrite -- 197 commits (8.6%) clear 200 removed lines, 98 clear 500 --
#      most of which add as much as they remove. Rejected: that is the
#      cry-wolf shape.
#
#   2. NET lines (removed minus added) alone. Cleaner on refactors, but
#      `44f71a6cc` is net +9,709: it deletes 27 files and adds a great deal
#      more elsewhere in the same commit, so net says the change GREW. A
#      net-only guard is blind to exactly the removal that is hardest to see in
#      review -- one buried in a big feature diff. Rejected, and T2 exists
#      because of it.
#
#   3. Accepting a bare `#123` anywhere in the description as the citation
#      (which is how the TODO.md entry that asked for this guard first framed
#      it). GitHub's squash-merge subject line ends in `(#49)` on every single
#      merged PR in this repo, so a bare issue reference would auto-clear every
#      change and the guard would be decorative from day one. Hence the
#      explicit `Large-deletion:` trailer: an issue number counts, but only
#      where someone typed it on purpose.
#
# A fourth was rejected on principle rather than measurement: keying the
# exemption on "is this path cloud?" the way script/check_cloud_boundary does.
# The four removals this guard exists for were all LOCAL subsystems, and three
# of the four were initially mis-framed as cloud. A cloud-shaped exemption
# would have cleared them.
#
# WHY IT CANNOT SILENTLY DISARM
# -----------------------------
# Without a base commit there is nothing to diff, and the honest answer is
# "cannot check". Locally that exits 0 with a diagnostic, so a shallow clone or
# a detached worktree does not block anyone. In CI that reads identically to
# "nothing to do", which is precisely how the pr-check oracle fetch stayed
# broken for weeks while reporting green -- so CI passes --require-base, which
# turns an unresolvable base into a hard failure.
#
# USAGE
#     script/check_large_deletions                  # vs merge-base with origin/main, incl. worktree
#     script/check_large_deletions BASE             # vs BASE, incl. worktree
#     script/check_large_deletions BASE HEAD        # a committed range (used to audit history)
#     script/check_large_deletions --require-base   # unresolvable base is an error, not a skip
# LC_ALL=C on sorts is deliberate: under a UTF-8 locale `sort` ignores
# punctuation when collating, so tokens differing only by a leading '#'
# (or similar) compare EQUAL and `sort -u` silently drops one. That made
# check_sweep_ledger pass locally and fail in CI on 2026-08-19.

set -uo pipefail
cd "$(dirname "$0")/.."

# --- thresholds. See "THRESHOLDS, AND THE MEASUREMENT THAT SET THEM" above.
# Deliberately not environment-overridable: an env knob on a guard is a disarm
# switch, and this one already has a documented, reviewable escape hatch.
LINES_REMOVED_LIMIT=750
WHOLE_FILE_LINES_LIMIT=1500

ALLOWLIST_FILE="script/large_deletion_allowlist.txt"

require_base=0
args=()
for a in "$@"; do
    case "$a" in
    --require-base) require_base=1 ;;
    -h | --help)
        sed -n '2,150p' "$0" | sed 's/^# \{0,1\}//;s/^#$//'
        exit 0
        ;;
    -*)
        echo "check_large_deletions: unknown option $a" >&2
        exit 2
        ;;
    *) args+=("$a") ;;
    esac
done

base="${args[0]:-${PHOSPHOR_DELETION_BASE:-}}"
head_rev="${args[1]:-}"

if [[ -z "$base" ]]; then
    if git rev-parse --verify --quiet origin/main >/dev/null; then
        base="$(git merge-base HEAD origin/main 2>/dev/null)" || base=""
    fi
fi

if [[ -z "$base" ]] || ! git rev-parse --verify --quiet "${base}^{commit}" >/dev/null; then
    if [[ "$require_base" -eq 1 ]]; then
        echo "error: no usable base commit to diff against (looked at argv, \$PHOSPHOR_DELETION_BASE, merge-base with origin/main)" >&2
        echo "       --require-base was passed, so this is a failure rather than a skip." >&2
        exit 2
    fi
    echo "large deletions: SKIPPED -- no base commit to diff against (pass one as \$1)"
    exit 0
fi
base="$(git rev-parse "$base")"

# --- allowlist. One bash glob per line; `#` comments and blanks ignored.
# Matching is `[[ path == pattern ]]`, where `*` DOES cross `/` -- so `docs/*`
# covers `docs/a/b.md`. A trailing `/` means "this directory and everything
# under it".
allow_patterns=()
if [[ -f "$ALLOWLIST_FILE" ]]; then
    while IFS= read -r line; do
        line="${line%%$'\r'}"
        [[ -z "${line// /}" || "$line" == \#* ]] && continue
        allow_patterns+=("$line")
    done < "$ALLOWLIST_FILE"
fi

is_allowed() {
    local path="$1" pat
    for pat in "${allow_patterns[@]:-}"; do
        [[ -z "$pat" ]] && continue
        if [[ "$pat" == */ ]]; then
            [[ "$path" == "$pat"* ]] && return 0
        else
            # shellcheck disable=SC2053  # glob match is the point
            [[ "$path" == $pat ]] && return 0
        fi
    done
    return 1
}

# --- collect the numbers.
#
# `-z` rather than plain `--numstat` is not cosmetic, and getting this wrong
# already cost one round of this script. git quotes paths containing spaces or
# non-ASCII, and this repo has both (a commit history written in Chinese, and
# `docs/OpenWarp Plan <CJK>.md`). A whitespace-split parser reads
# `"docs/OpenWarp` as the path, so the `docs/` allowlist entry silently stops
# matching. The threshold measurement was FIRST RUN with such a parser and it
# produced two wrong conclusions that went straight into the header above:
# `93e738bb9` scored as a 4,445-line source deletion when it deletes one
# markdown file, and `b0b1faef9` scored as +4,716/-2,794 -- "a subsystem
# removal bundled with a bigger feature" -- when it is really +271/-2,794 and
# 4,445 of those adds were the same class of CJK-named markdown. The real
# bundling case is `44f71a6cc`, and T2's threshold had to be re-derived.
added=0
removed=0
whole_removed=0
whole_files=0
deleted_paths=()
skipped_paths=0

scan() {
    local filter="$1" a d p rec
    while IFS= read -r -d '' rec; do
        a="${rec%%$'\t'*}"
        rec="${rec#*$'\t'}"
        d="${rec%%$'\t'*}"
        p="${rec#*$'\t'}"
        if [[ -z "$p" ]]; then
            # Rename/copy: the two NUL-terminated paths follow. -M means this
            # is a move, not a deletion, so it must not count either way.
            IFS= read -r -d '' _old || true
            IFS= read -r -d '' p || true
        fi
        [[ "$a" == "-" || "$d" == "-" ]] && continue # binary
        if is_allowed "$p"; then
            # Counted on the "all" pass only -- the D pass revisits the same
            # paths and would double-count them in the summary line.
            [[ "$filter" != "D" ]] && skipped_paths=$((skipped_paths + 1))
            continue
        fi
        if [[ "$filter" == "D" ]]; then
            whole_removed=$((whole_removed + d))
            whole_files=$((whole_files + 1))
            deleted_paths+=("$p")
        else
            added=$((added + a))
            removed=$((removed + d))
        fi
    done
}

if [[ -n "$head_rev" ]]; then
    range=("$base" "$head_rev")
else
    range=("$base")
fi

scan all < <(git diff -z --numstat -M "${range[@]}" -- 2>/dev/null)
scan D < <(git diff -z --numstat -M --diff-filter=D "${range[@]}" -- 2>/dev/null)

t1=0
t2=0
[[ "$removed" -ge "$LINES_REMOVED_LIMIT" && $((added * 2)) -lt "$removed" ]] && t1=1
[[ "$whole_removed" -ge "$WHOLE_FILE_LINES_LIMIT" ]] && t2=1

summary="$(printf '+%d/-%d lines, %d file(s) deleted outright (-%d lines), %d path(s) allowlisted' \
    "$added" "$removed" "$whole_files" "$whole_removed" "$skipped_paths")"

if [[ "$t1" -eq 0 && "$t2" -eq 0 ]]; then
    echo "large deletions: ok (${summary}; base ${base:0:9})"
    exit 0
fi

# --- a trigger fired. Look for the explanation.
description="$(git log --format='%B' "${base}..${head_rev:-HEAD}" 2>/dev/null || true)"
description+=$'\n'"${PR_TITLE:-}"$'\n'"${PR_BODY:-}"

cites_declined=0
has_trailer=0
# The DECLINED.md citation must appear in a commit that actually DELETES
# something, or in the PR title/body. Previously this grepped every commit
# message in base..HEAD at once, so a single incidental mention anywhere in the
# range -- including a docs commit that merely referenced the file -- cleared any
# bulk deletion elsewhere in the same range. That is a substring test over
# unrelated commits, not an explanation.
#
# The Large-deletion: trailer keeps its range-wide reading deliberately: it is an
# explicit, purpose-built opt-in that nobody writes by accident, whereas
# "DECLINED.md" is a filename that appears in ordinary prose constantly.
# Select commits that REMOVE something -- lines or whole files. `--diff-filter=D`
# alone was wrong and regressed this guard on 2026-08-21: trigger T1 fires on
# deleted LINE COUNT and can reach its threshold with zero whole-file deletions,
# in which case --diff-filter=D returns nothing, the description is empty, and a
# commit that DOES cite DECLINED.md is never read. That made remedy #1 in this
# guard's own error text unreachable for T1. Verified against a synthetic repo:
# one commit deleting 1,700 lines from a surviving file, message citing
# DECLINED.md, went from "ok -- explained" to a hard failure.
#
# numstat's second column is deletions; any commit with a non-zero, non-binary
# deletion count qualifies.
deleting_commits=""
while IFS= read -r sha; do
    [[ -z "$sha" ]] && continue
    # A commit qualifies only if it is NET-DELETING and substantially so.
    #
    # An earlier version accepted any commit whose deletion column was non-zero,
    # which reopened the very defect this scoping exists to close: a MODIFIED
    # line counts as one addition and one deletion, so rewriting a single README
    # line to say "see DECLINED.md" qualified the commit and cleared a 1,700-line
    # deletion elsewhere in the range. Verified by adversarial probe.
    #
    # DECLINED.md and TODO.md are excluded from the tally outright -- editing the
    # ledger must never be what makes a commit count as a deletion.
    if git show --numstat --format='' "$sha" 2>/dev/null \
        | awk '
            NF >= 3 && $2 != "-" && $1 != "-" {
                path = $3
                if (path == "DECLINED.md" || path == "TODO.md") next
                add += $1; del += $2
            }
            END { exit !(del > add && del >= 20) }
        '; then
        deleting_commits+="$sha"$'\n'
    fi
done <<< "$(git log --format='%H' "${base}..${head_rev:-HEAD}" 2>/dev/null || true)"

deleting_description=""
if [[ -n "${deleting_commits//[[:space:]]/}" ]]; then
    deleting_description="$(git log --format='%B' --no-walk $deleting_commits 2>/dev/null || true)"
fi
deleting_description+=$'\n'"${PR_TITLE:-}"$'\n'"${PR_BODY:-}"
grep -qF 'DECLINED.md' <<< "$deleting_description" && cites_declined=1
grep -qE '^[[:space:]]*Large-deletion:[[:space:]]*[^[:space:]]' <<< "$description" && has_trailer=1

# There is deliberately NO third "the allowlist covers it" branch here. The
# allowlist works by exclusion at scan time, so an allowlisted path never
# reaches a counter and can never be part of the reason a trigger fired. An
# earlier draft carried an `all_deletions_allowlisted` clause as well; it was
# unreachable-by-construction dead logic that only made the guard look more
# forgiving than it is. If a firing here is genuinely structural, the fix is to
# add the path to the allowlist, not to add another way past this check.
if [[ "$cites_declined" -eq 1 || "$has_trailer" -eq 1 ]]; then
    why="cites DECLINED.md"
    [[ "$has_trailer" -eq 1 ]] && why="has a Large-deletion: trailer"
    echo "large deletions: ok -- explained (${why}) (${summary}; base ${base:0:9})"
    exit 0
fi

{
    echo "error: this change removes a lot of code and does not say why."
    echo
    printf '  base ............... %s\n' "${base:0:9}"
    printf '  lines +/- .......... +%d / -%d\n' "$added" "$removed"
    printf '  deleted outright ... %d file(s), -%d lines\n' "$whole_files" "$whole_removed"
    [[ "$t1" -eq 1 ]] && printf '  TRIGGER T1 ......... >= %d lines removed and >2x more removed than added\n' "$LINES_REMOVED_LIMIT"
    [[ "$t2" -eq 1 ]] && printf '  TRIGGER T2 ......... >= %d lines removed by outright file deletion\n' "$WHOLE_FILE_LINES_LIMIT"
    if [[ "${#deleted_paths[@]}" -gt 0 ]]; then
        echo
        echo "  files deleted outright:"
        printf '    %s\n' "${deleted_paths[@]}" | LC_ALL=C sort | head -40
        [[ "${#deleted_paths[@]}" -gt 40 ]] && printf '    ... and %d more\n' $(("${#deleted_paths[@]}" - 40))
    fi
    cat <<'EOF'

Four removals of local subsystems landed in this fork with no record anywhere,
were found months later by accident, and two had to be restored (TODO.md,
"Four deliberate removals"). This guard exists so there is never a fifth. It is
not asking you to justify the removal to a script -- it is asking you to write
the removal down where a reviewer will see it.

Clear it with ANY ONE of:

  1. Say what decision this implements, by naming DECLINED.md in the commit
     message or PR body -- and add the row if it is genuinely a new decision.

  2. Add a trailer line to the commit message or PR body:

         Large-deletion: removes the X subsystem, refs #1234

     An issue number, a DECLINED.md row, or a plain sentence all count. It has
     to be typed on purpose: a bare `(#49)` in a squash subject does not.

  3. If the deletion is structural (vendored code, generated output, lockfiles,
     scratch), add the path to script/large_deletion_allowlist.txt WITH A
     COMMENT saying why. That file may shrink freely; growing it is a claim
     that the path is not fork behaviour, and needs the same scrutiny as any
     other boundary change (AGENTS.md 5.10).

Do NOT clear this by splitting the removal across several commits so each one
lands under the limit. That defeats the guard and is worse than the original
gap, because the diff then looks routine.
EOF
} >&2
exit 1
